<article class="insight"><div class="label">01 · Concentration</div><h3>Chip exposure dominates the list</h3><p>Four of these ten funds explicitly target semiconductors, alongside an AI themed fund. Different tickers can still own many of the same chipmakers, so ticker count overstates diversification. Compare holdings overlap and fund concentration before treating them as separate bets.</p></article>
<article class="insight"><div class="label">02 · Broader leadership</div><h3>Energy activity has widened</h3><p>Refiners, shipping and oil-and-gas producers appear alongside the chip rally. These funds track distinct businesses and can react differently to crude prices, freight rates and refining margins; a shared “energy” label does not mean shared drivers.</p></article>
<article class="insight"><div class="label">03 · Global supply chain</div><h3>Taiwan ETFs add geographic exposure</h3><p>FLTW and EWT both offer Taiwan equity exposure. Their gains may reflect semiconductor supply-chain strength, but add country, currency and geopolitical risks. Check holdings overlap: the two funds are not necessarily independent exposures.</p></article>
<article class="insight"><div class="label">04 · How to read momentum</div><h3>Return alone leaves out the ride</h3><p>YTD performance doesn’t show drawdowns, volatility, liquidity, fees or valuation. Compare 1-, 3- and 12-month returns and relative performance versus a broad benchmark; verify fund size, spreads and current holdings with the issuer.</p></article></section>
